## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-region ordering, coordination, failover recovery, and durable state management in a realistic KCNA-like system.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant global sequence
- Cross-region coordination
- Durable storage and idempotent processing
- Reordering buffers for in-order emission
- Failure/recovery paths and testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal sketch
class Coordinator {
  constructor(store) { this.store = store; }
  allocate(tenantId) {
    const last = this.store[tenantId]?.lastSeq || 0;
    const next = last + 1;
    this.store[tenantId] = { lastSeq: next };
    return next;
  }
  commit(tenantId, seq) {
    // durable commit placeholder
    this.store[tenantId].lastSeq = Math.max(this.store[tenantId].lastSeq, seq);
  }
}
class Consumer {
  constructor(coord) { this.coord = coord; this.buff = new Map(); }
  onMessage(msg) {
    const { tenantId, globalSeq } = msg.meta;
    if (!this.buff.has(tenantId)) this.buff.set(tenantId, new Map());
    const map = this.buff.get(tenantId);
    map.set(globalSeq, msg);
    this.flush(tenantId);
  }
  flush(tenantId) {
    const map = this.buff.get(tenantId);
    let seq = this.coord.peekNext(tenantId);
    while (map.has(seq)) {
      const m = map.get(seq);
      // process(m)
      map.delete(seq);
      seq++;
      this.coord.commit(tenantId, seq);
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle clock skew and coordinator failover?
- What metrics would you surface for latency and duplicates?

## Why This Is Asked

Auditors require end-to-end data provenance in KCNA with tenant isolation, cryptographic integrity, and scalable verification. This question tests how to design lightweight, verifiable lineage without payload leakage and how to handle dynamic rotation and maintenance under failure.

## Key Concepts

- Data provenance and per-tenant isolation
- Cryptographic hash chaining and Merkle trees
- Envelope encryption and retention policies
- At-least-once semantics and failure recovery

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight Merkle root builder (conceptual)
class MerkleTree {
  constructor(){ this.leaves = []; this.rootHash = null; }
  addLeaf(data) { this.leaves.push(hash(data)); this.rootHash = this.computeRoot(); }
  computeRoot(){ // simplistic binary Merkle root placeholder
    let nodes = this.leaves.slice();
    while (nodes.length > 1) {
      const next = [];
      for (let i = 0; i < nodes.length; i += 2) {
        const a = nodes[i];
        const b = nodes[i+1] || a;
        next.push(hash(a + b));
      }
      nodes = next;
    }
    return nodes[0] || null;
  }
}
function hash(x){ // placeholder for a real crypto hash
  return require('crypto').createHash('sha256').update(x).digest('hex');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale Merkle trees across thousands of tenants without cross-tenant leakage?
- How do you rotate keys without invalidating existing proofs, and what is the audit protocol for retroactive verifications?

